content.text:
`tech-domain` triggered because idea "A simple to-do list with no AI, no database, and no deployment." The phrase "no deployment" matches `deploy\w*` public-deployment. That's a false positive from negation. Need handle negations or change test idea. Better engine should avoid negated needs. Implement a simple negation filter: for each need, find pattern match; if within preceding ~20 chars there's "no ", "without ", "not ", skip. But "no deployment" at end, preceding "no " immediate. Similarly "no AI", "no database" should not trigger. This improves quality. Implement extraction with per-need pattern exec and negation check.

Current `extractNeeds` uses `.test`. Rewrite:
```
function hasNeed(text, pattern) {
 pattern.lastIndex = 0;
 const match = pattern.exec(text);
 if (!match) return false;
 const prefix = text.slice(Math.max(0, match.index-24), match.index).toLowerCase();
 if (/\b(no|not|without|never|zero)\s+$/.test(prefix)) return false;
 return true;
}
```
But "no deployment" prefix "no " -> false. "no database" -> remote-database pattern maybe doesn't match "database" alone? It requires remote/cloud/managed/postgres/mongodb/mysql/atlas/multi-user/shared data; "database" not. local-database pattern not. Fine. "no AI" -> ai-inference pattern `ai` matches at "AI", prefix "no " -> false. Good.
But phrases like "no proprietary" etc no effect.
`extractExistingStack` similarly could pick up "no OpenCode"? Not relevant.
`extractConstraints` noSpend true for "no spending". Good.

Implement.

input.newString:
function mentionsNeed(text: string, pattern: RegExp): boolean {
  const match = pattern.exec(text);
  if (!match) return false;
  const prefix = text.slice(Math.max(0, match.index - 24), match.index).toLowerCase();
  // Treat "no deployment", "without AI", etc. as absent needs rather than matches.
  return !/\b(no|not|without|never|zero)\s+$/.test(prefix);
}

export function extractNeeds(text: string): ToolCategory[] {
  const found = NEED_PATTERNS
    .filter(([, pattern]) => mentionsNeed(text, pattern))
    .map(([need]) => need);
  return NEED_ORDER.filter((need) => found.includes(need));
}

